    "text_delta": "r insinuations of gender bias based solely on the gender of the panel carries substantial risks:\n* **Professional reputation:** The civil service is a close-knit environment. Raising an allegation without factual evidence can harm working relationships and professional standing.\n* **Weak administrative standing:** Formal appeals or grievances in civil service systems require demonstrating a breach of merit principles or unlawful discrimination. Citing the panel's gender composition alone is unlikely to be considered valid grounds for appeal.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite follow-up:**  \n   Send a brief, professional note to HR or the recruitment coordinator thanking them again for the opportunity and reiterating your request for constructive feedback to support your professional development.\n2. **Focus questions on competencies:**  \n   When you receive feedback, focus on specific areas:\n   * How was your interview performance evaluated against the scoring framework?\n   * Were there specific competencies or experiences the panel felt could be strengthened?\n3. **Recognize your strong positioning:**  \n   Placing in the top three for a senior policy role demonstrates that you are highly competitive. With constructive feedback, your profile remains very strong for upcoming senior vacancies.\n",
